- I want Jaemin's braised kimchi. - All of a sudden?

258
00:08:30,310 --> 00:08:31,396
Since we are talking about kimchi.

259
00:08:31,420 --> 00:08:32,420
Braised kimchi.

260
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:35,520
- Braised kimchi is my favorite. - Really?

261
00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:37,219
- I said that I loved it. - Braised kimchi.

262
00:08:37,220 --> 00:08:38,260
That's right.

263
00:08:38,620 --> 00:08:40,630
(That's my specialty!)

264
00:08:40,830 --> 00:08:42,910
- She draws really well, but... - She's good, but...

265
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:44,080
it's not quite recognizable.

266
00:08:44,330 --> 00:08:45,429
- Recognizable... - I mean...

267
00:08:45,430 --> 00:08:47,630
- Is the meat that small? - That's kimchi.

268
00:08:47,730 --> 00:08:49,170
Jaemin, you leaned all the way over.

269
00:08:49,670 --> 00:08:51,176
Isn't it omelet rice? The one on the left.

270
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:52,276
- You're right. - Omelet rice.

271
00:08:52,300 --> 00:08:54,116
The dish she wants to share the recipe for the whole world...

272
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:55,339
- is omelet rice. - The vibe of the omelet rice...

273
00:08:55,340 --> 00:08:56,640
is definitely different.

274
00:08:56,710 --> 00:08:59,979
It's not just any omelet rice. It's the omelet rice my dad makes,

275
00:08:59,980 --> 00:09:01,410
so I wanted to introduce it.

276
00:09:01,610 --> 00:09:03,610
Does your father make omelet rice for you himself?

277
00:09:03,710 --> 00:09:06,320
Ever since I was in elementary school,

278
00:09:06,420 --> 00:09:07,420
- he would make it... - Really?

279
00:09:07,421 --> 00:09:08,450
For me every night.

280
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:09,520
Every night?

281
00:09:09,590 --> 00:09:10,896
Did he draw hearts on it like this?

282
00:09:10,920 --> 00:09:12,266
- Yes, he'd draw hearts, - Really?

283
00:09:12,290 --> 00:09:15,290
And he has his own recipe. When I was a kid,

284
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:16,830
it always had to be made by my dad.

285
00:09:17,030 --> 00:09:18,030
Let's guess this one.

286
00:09:18,130 --> 00:09:19,900
- Motsunabe. - But...

287
00:09:19,960 --> 00:09:22,600
- Vegetarian soup? - It's close.

288
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:24,940
I thought he was casting a Harry Potter spell.

289
00:09:25,370 --> 00:09:26,490
- Vegetarian soup. - It's...

290
00:09:26,770 --> 00:09:28,639
Expecto Patronum, zoom, zoom.

291
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:30,309
Vegetarian soup.

292
00:09:30,310 --> 00:09:33,210
Well, soup is... I mean, it does belong to the soup category.

293
00:09:33,410 --> 00:09:36,709
- Then it must be stew. - Right. Stew, stew.

294
00:09:36,710 --> 00:09:40,380
- Stew, stew. - Well, Japanese people often...

295
00:09:40,450 --> 00:09:42,720
make curry and stew at home.

296
00:09:43,020 --> 00:09:46,820
Whenever winter came, my mom always made stew for us.

297
00:09:46,920 --> 00:09:48,480
And there are different ways to eat it.

298
00:09:48,530 --> 00:09:50,330
- Like what? - Some people eat it with rice,

299
00:09:50,430 --> 00:09:51,700
while other people dip...

300
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:52,936
- bread into it. - With bread?

301
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:54,206
I've seen people eat it with bread a lot.

302
00:09:54,230 --> 00:09:55,800
I actually eat it both ways.

303
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:59,600
- Dipping and scraping it up. - Yes, yes, that's right.

304
00:09:59,740 --> 00:10:03,870
- Great job. - Well, now we've learned...

305
00:10:03,940 --> 00:10:06,380
all about Minami's tastes. Awesome.

306
00:10:06,510 --> 00:10:08,280
Today's theme...

307
00:10:08,350 --> 00:10:11,120
is the nostalgic taste of school food.

308
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:12,720
(School Food)

309
00:10:13,120 --> 00:10:15,389
Since we're all dressed in school looks today,

310
00:10:15,390 --> 00:10:16,596
let's take a look at our outfits...

311
00:10:16,620 --> 00:10:18,336
Carmen, you said this is a school uniform...

312
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:19,720
from Bali, Indonesia, right?

313
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:21,930
Yes, it's a real uniform. I brought it from Bali.

314
00:10:22,030 --> 00:10:23,160
- Really? - Yes.

315
00:10:23,230 --> 00:10:26,130
- That's incredible. - The dedication is insane.

316
00:10:27,530 --> 00:10:29,130
- It's really... - It's so pretty.

317
00:10:29,300 --> 00:10:31,100
I feel like I've seen this in movies.

318
00:10:31,370 --> 00:10:33,770
- It's so pretty. - Carmen, you still really look...

319
00:10:33,840 --> 00:10:34,840
like a student.

320
00:10:35,210 --> 00:10:36,810
Wow, I'm jealous.

321
00:10:38,010 --> 00:10:41,480
You know what, Carmen? In Korea, we used to hem our skirts.

322
00:10:41,550 --> 00:10:42,880
It was the trend back then,

323
00:10:42,950 --> 00:10:44,630
so we had to walk up the stairs like this.

324
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:47,120
(Like this)

325
00:10:47,320 --> 00:10:49,120
Shortening them was such a trend.

326
00:10:50,520 --> 00:10:52,220
Minami's uniform is so pretty too.

327
00:10:52,290 --> 00:10:54,266
Right. I tried to find one as close as possible...

328
00:10:54,290 --> 00:10:56,130
- I've seen this a lot in anime. - Totally.

329
00:10:56,190 --> 00:10:57,266
I've seen it a lot in manga.

330
00:10:57,290 --> 00:10:58,600
True.

331
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:00,359
- Japanese anime. - Exactly.

332
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:02,570
Japanese uniforms are uniquely pretty.

333
00:11:02,630 --> 00:11:04,539
- They're pretty. - For both guys and girls.

334
00:11:04,540 --> 00:11:05,870
First, the skirt has to be long.

335
00:11:05,940 --> 00:11:06,940
It has to be long.

336
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,209
It has to reach your knees. If it's above that,

337
00:11:10,210 --> 00:11:12,310
you get in trouble.

338
00:11:12,910 --> 00:11:15,710
So in your case, you would've been busted right away.

339
00:11:18,520 --> 00:11:20,680
Wearing uniforms really makes it so perfect for us...

340
00:11:20,750 --> 00:11:22,950
to talk about our school days...

341
00:11:23,020 --> 00:11:25,820
Carmen, what kind of student were you back then?

342
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:28,029
What kind of student do you think I was?

343
00:11:28,030 --> 00:11:30,229
(What kind of student do you think I was?)

344
00:11:30,230 --> 00:11:31,306
(Quizzing them instead of answering)

345
00:11:31,330 --> 00:11:32,330
- Gosh. - As expected.

346
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:34,829
(Please guess about my school days.)

347
00:11:34,830 --> 00:11:36,900
Eun Ji. Gym class leader. Gym class leader.

348
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:38,270
No, no. In my opinion,

349
00:11:38,340 --> 00:11:41,710
she has a prominent forehead.

350
00:11:42,110 --> 00:11:44,016
- People like that study well. - Were you good at studying?

351
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:45,486
- I bet she was. - I bet she was good at it.

352
00:11:45,510 --> 00:11:46,686
That's right, I was good at studying.

353
00:11:46,710 --> 00:11:51,280
- I was second or third in my class. - Second or third? How many...

354
00:11:51,350 --> 00:11:52,750
students were usually in one class?

355
00:11:52,820 --> 00:11:54,590
- Around 34? - Really?

356
00:11:54,650 --> 00:11:55,720
You were smart.

357
00:11:56,050 --> 00:11:57,819
- I was best at history. - History.

358
00:11:57,820 --> 00:11:59,366
So I entered a lot of history competitions.

359
00:11:59,390 --> 00:12:00,960
- In history? - Competitions?

360
00:12:02,430 --> 00:12:03,590
I was good at math,

361
00:12:03,660 --> 00:12:05,930
- so I competed in math olympiads. - Really?

362
00:12:06,430 --> 00:12:08,030
Well, you're "from an olympiad..."

363
00:12:08,330 --> 00:12:10,530
- just by entering one. - True, true.

364
00:12:10,600 --> 00:12:11,976
Anyone can enter if they pay the fee.

365
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:13,276
You can enter if you pay, but still,

366
00:12:13,300 --> 00:12:15,286
- not just anyone enters. - You need a recommendation first.

367
00:12:15,310 --> 00:12:16,769
- Yes, so... - You need a recommendation.

368
00:12:16,770 --> 00:12:19,009
(Anyway, he competed in olympiads.)

369
00:12:19,010 --> 00:12:21,109
Among our IDOL'S TABLE family,

370
00:12:21,110 --> 00:12:23,049
I heard that we have one more member...

371
00:12:23,050 --> 00:12:25,220
- who was really good at studying. - Is that so?

372
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:28,420
- I have a feeling. - I have a feeling.

373
00:12:28,920 --> 00:12:32,519
- I think it is Chenle. - Well, before I came to Korea,

374
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:33,590
back in middle school,

375
00:12:33,660 --> 00:12:36,100
I was almost always ranked first in the whole school in math.

376
00:12:36,130 --> 00:12:37,436
- Really? - Always perfect scores.

377
00:12:37,460 --> 00:12:38,700
- You were good at math? - Yes.

378
00:12:38,730 --> 00:12:39,890
That is so far from my world.

379
00:12:40,030 --> 00:12:41,950
Yes, and I was good at English too. For English,

380
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:44,216
I was always ranked in the top five in the whole school.

381
00:12:44,240 --> 00:12:45,240
That is cool.

382
00:12:45,970 --> 00:12:47,116
I feel like Jaemin was good at studying.

383
00:12:47,140 --> 00:12:49,169
Me? No.

384
00:12:49,170 --> 00:12:51,610
(No.)

385
00:12:52,310 --> 00:12:53,780
I was not into studying.

386
00:12:54,210 --> 00:12:55,210
Really?

387
00:12:55,480 --> 00:12:56,556
(His face is just like an honor student.)

388
00:12:56,580 --> 00:12:57,856
Did you not have girls like that?

389
00:12:57,880 --> 00:13:00,679
Someone screaming, "Jaemin" when Jaemin went to school.

390
00:13:00,680 --> 00:13:02,820
- Schoolgirls screaming like that. - Did you?

391
00:13:03,320 --> 00:13:06,960
No, I did not, but the older girls liked me.

392
00:13:07,020 --> 00:13:08,589
- Older girls, right? It must be. - Upperclassman.

393
00:13:08,590 --> 00:13:09,660
Yes, upperclassman.

394
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:12,860
Usually, on days like Valentine's Day...

395
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:14,599
You open your locker, gifts pour out.

396
00:13:14,600 --> 00:13:16,406
There was nothing pouring out. Nothing like that.

397
00:13:16,430 --> 00:13:20,670
- No, really. - Do you know origami lucky stars?

398
00:13:20,770 --> 00:13:23,610
- Right, right, those exist. - Folding paper into stars...

399
00:13:23,670 --> 00:13:24,809
Yes, I received a lot of those.

400
00:13:24,810 --> 00:13:26,056
- Really? - Did you get a lot, Chenle?

401
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:27,256
Hey, that was our generation's thing.

402
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:32,080
Wait, Chenle, did you bring that up just to talk about yourself?

403
00:13:32,820 --> 00:13:35,581
- Just to brag that he got some. - He just wanted to say he got some.

404
00:13:36,350 --> 00:13:38,336
How were the school days for our new member, Minami?

405
00:13:38,360 --> 00:13:40,860
I used to go to an English-language kindergarten.

406
00:13:41,260 --> 00:13:43,930
- An international one like that. - Me too.

407
00:13:43,990 --> 00:13:45,629
Since I only spoke English there,

408
00:13:45,630 --> 00:13:46,699
my mom said, "If you are going to learn English,"

409
00:13:46,700 --> 00:13:48,146
- "you need to know Japanese first." - I get that.

410
00:13:48,170 --> 00:13:49,870
That is what my mom told me,

411
00:13:50,170 --> 00:13:52,469
so we decided I should go to a Japanese school,

412
00:13:52,470 --> 00:13:53,676
and that was when I learned Japanese...

413
00:13:53,700 --> 00:13:55,070
for the first time.

414
00:13:55,470 --> 00:13:57,980
- You learned Japanese back then? - Yes.

415
00:13:58,040 --> 00:13:59,686
You did not know how to speak Japanese before that?

416
00:13:59,710 --> 00:14:01,280
- Not really. - That's crazy.

417
00:14:01,350 --> 00:14:02,726
- English felt more comfortable... - English was...

418
00:14:02,750 --> 00:14:04,656
- I forgot a lot of it now, though. - That is crazy.

419
00:14:04,680 --> 00:14:08,889
But in middle school, on speech tests,

420
00:14:08,890 --> 00:14:10,550
I got perfect scores,

421
00:14:11,260 --> 00:14:12,736
and I was even ranked first in the whole school.

422
00:14:12,760 --> 00:14:13,760
Wow.

423
00:14:14,460 --> 00:14:18,229
By the way, how long have you lived here in Korea?

424
00:14:18,230 --> 00:14:20,060
It's been five years now.

425
00:14:21,070 --> 00:14:22,110
Why are you so good at it?

426
00:14:22,170 --> 00:14:23,546
Seriously, why is your Korean so good?

427
00:14:23,570 --> 00:14:25,176
I think it's because I enjoy learning languages.

428
00:14:25,200 --> 00:14:27,770
I'm jealous that she feels like that. I mean, we...

429
00:14:28,070 --> 00:14:30,670
We had to learn it, didn't we? Honestly, we had no choice.

430
00:14:32,080 --> 00:14:34,480
Why are you lumping us together?

431
00:14:34,580 --> 00:14:35,580
- Why are you putting me... - I had...

432
00:14:35,581 --> 00:14:36,656
- no choice. - in the same boat?

433
00:14:36,680 --> 00:14:37,680
Not me.

434
00:14:38,420 --> 00:14:40,050
Not the same? Okay.

435
00:14:40,120 --> 00:14:41,189
I was just looking for my allies.

436
00:14:41,190 --> 00:14:42,650
(Laughing)

437
00:14:42,750 --> 00:14:45,990
Minami can even ride a unicycle.

438
00:14:46,760 --> 00:14:49,060
- A unicycle? - What is a unicycle? Oh, the one...

439
00:14:49,260 --> 00:14:50,260
One wheel?

440
00:14:50,330 --> 00:14:51,650
Only clowns can ride that, right?

441
00:14:51,860 --> 00:14:54,629
Kind of like that, but going backward like this...

442
00:14:54,630 --> 00:14:55,670
- Backward too? - Yes.

443
00:14:56,270 --> 00:14:57,770
Like going forward and then backward.

444
00:14:57,970 --> 00:15:00,340
In Japan, almost all students learn to ride a unicycle,

445
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:01,486
just like kids in Korea learn how to do jump rope.

446
00:15:01,510 --> 00:15:03,786
- Almost all students learn that. - It's kind of like that.

447
00:15:03,810 --> 00:15:04,810
Really?

448
00:15:05,310 --> 00:15:06,756
I want to try. I think I'd be good at it.

449
00:15:06,780 --> 00:15:07,879
Try it. You'd be good.

450
00:15:07,880 --> 00:15:09,156
Wait, I think you'd be good at it.

451
00:15:09,180 --> 00:15:10,820
I want you to spin plates while doing it.

452
00:15:11,080 --> 00:15:12,440
- Like juggling. - Plates, plates.

453
00:15:12,880 --> 00:15:15,390
- Film it for content. - You'd do great.

454
00:15:15,450 --> 00:15:16,826
I'll shoot some content of me doing this.

455
00:15:16,850 --> 00:15:18,920
- Yes, sounds great. - That'll be fun.

456
00:15:19,860 --> 00:15:23,360
Why don't we play an image game just for fun among ourselves?

457
00:15:23,660 --> 00:15:25,560
- We can ask questions... - Sounds good.

458
00:15:25,630 --> 00:15:29,570
- Sounds good. Love it. - Here it comes.

459
00:15:29,970 --> 00:15:31,976
My homeroom teacher used to hold this all the time.

460
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:33,000
This?

461
00:15:33,001 --> 00:15:34,170
(Poking)

462
00:15:34,810 --> 00:15:36,870
- Hey, hey, hey. - What are you doing?

463
00:15:37,270 --> 00:15:38,279
(Please focus.)

464
00:15:38,280 --> 00:15:42,250
- I think it'll be fun. - All right, let me ask a question.

465
00:15:42,310 --> 00:15:43,750
- Okay. - In the cafeteria,

466
00:15:43,810 --> 00:15:44,820
In the cafeteria...

467
00:15:44,880 --> 00:15:50,649
who would save the most delicious side dish until the end?

468
00:15:50,650 --> 00:15:52,259
- Ready. One, two, three. - One, two, three.

469
00:15:52,260 --> 00:15:53,859
(If you are ready, point!)

470
00:15:53,860 --> 00:15:55,259
- Look, look. Who is it? - Wait.

471
00:15:55,260 --> 00:15:57,699
- Wait. Two, two, and... - Two for me too.

472
00:15:57,700 --> 00:15:59,420
Ha Sung Woon and Chenle got two votes each.

473
00:15:59,460 --> 00:16:01,206
Chenle, did you actually save the best for last?

474
00:16:01,230 --> 00:16:02,790
Totally. I saved it until the very end.

475
00:16:03,270 --> 00:16:05,376
My parents always told me, "Eat the tastiest thing first,"

476
00:16:05,400 --> 00:16:07,399
- "or it'll get cold." - Right, right.

477
00:16:07,400 --> 00:16:08,986
They would always say that, but I was like, "No,"

478
00:16:09,010 --> 00:16:10,946
- "I want to save it for last." - Saving the best for last.

479
00:16:10,970 --> 00:16:11,980
It has changed a bit now.

480
00:16:12,040 --> 00:16:14,449
These days, my stomach has gotten smaller.

481
00:16:14,450 --> 00:16:16,580
So I eat the delicious food first,

482
00:16:17,080 --> 00:16:18,126
in case there is no room left.

483
00:16:18,150 --> 00:16:19,850
- Digestive issues. - Digestion issues.

484
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:21,750
So, Sung Woon, you also...

485
00:16:21,950 --> 00:16:24,990
I remember eating fast and lining up to get seconds.

486
00:16:25,360 --> 00:16:26,720
Yes, yes. I would eat quickly...

487
00:16:26,790 --> 00:16:29,660
- and hurry back to get more. - Is there anyone here who just...

488
00:16:29,760 --> 00:16:30,960
eats the tastiest thing first?

489
00:16:30,990 --> 00:16:32,759
- BamBam. - Me too.

490
00:16:32,760 --> 00:16:33,829
- Eat it first? - Yes.

491
00:16:33,830 --> 00:16:34,946
It is more touching if you eat it first.

492
00:16:34,970 --> 00:16:37,630
- It is more touching. - Right, that is true. Great, great.

493
00:16:37,700 --> 00:16:39,180
- Let us do it one more time. - Okay.

494
00:16:39,270 --> 00:16:43,970
Who is most likely to have been caught secretly eating snacks?

495
00:16:44,040 --> 00:16:45,580
- There is one person. - Here we go.

496
00:16:45,980 --> 00:16:47,380
One, two, three.

497
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:50,350
- Hey, Carmen has so many votes. - Wait.

498
00:16:50,410 --> 00:16:52,820
How many votes did Carmen get?

499
00:16:53,480 --> 00:16:56,189
- I always ate in secret. - So confident.

500
00:16:56,190 --> 00:16:57,550
What did you eat in secret?

501
00:16:58,160 --> 00:17:00,119
- Snacks, like chips. - Like chips.

502
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:02,859
Because if I just focus on class,

503
00:17:02,860 --> 00:17:04,090
it is so boring.

504
00:17:04,160 --> 00:17:06,060
- It is boring. - It is boring. So I...

505
00:17:07,360 --> 00:17:10,570
Any tips? How did you secretly eat? Let's say I am the teacher.

506
00:17:10,870 --> 00:17:12,270
"Okay, kids, let me start class."

507
00:17:12,570 --> 00:17:13,570
Here is a book.

508
00:17:13,770 --> 00:17:16,939
- Doing this... Carmen... - Yes?

509
00:17:16,940 --> 00:17:18,310
What are you doing?

510
00:17:18,380 --> 00:17:19,380
I didn't do anything.

511
00:17:20,380 --> 00:17:22,050
No, but with a book...

512
00:17:22,110 --> 00:17:23,386
Back in school, when I saw friends eat,

513
00:17:23,410 --> 00:17:26,480
they stood a book up like this, so nobody could see their mouth.

514
00:17:26,780 --> 00:17:29,750
- You really can't see it. - I would eat like this...

515
00:17:30,050 --> 00:17:31,536
(Skill: Slyly covering the mouth with a hand)

516
00:17:31,560 --> 00:17:33,089
- Cover it, cover it. - Yes, I covered my mouth.

517
00:17:33,090 --> 00:17:35,160
- That's quite natural. - Doing it like this...

518
00:17:37,360 --> 00:17:41,329
Anyway, the best part of school is lunchtime, right?

519
00:17:41,330 --> 00:17:42,770
That is right, yes.

520
00:17:42,970 --> 00:17:44,870
(Counting down the minutes together...)

521
00:17:45,070 --> 00:17:46,170
(It's lunchtime!)

522
00:17:46,270 --> 00:17:48,240
(A rocket-speed race fueled by hunger)

523
00:17:48,770 --> 00:17:50,646
(Memories of school meals eaten breathlessly after sprinting)

524
00:17:50,670 --> 00:17:52,780
Did everyone have a favorite menu item?

525
00:17:53,380 --> 00:17:54,986
- Sweet-and-sour pork. - Sweet-and-sour pork?

526
00:17:55,010 --> 00:17:56,156
They serve sweet-and-sour pork at school?

527
00:17:56,180 --> 00:17:59,080
Yes. It was so good that whenever they served it, I skipped the soup.

528
00:17:59,280 --> 00:18:00,556
To get more pork in the soup bowl.

529
00:18:00,580 --> 00:18:02,140
To get sweet and sour pork in the bowl?

530
00:18:02,250 --> 00:18:03,490
That's crazy.

531
00:18:03,550 --> 00:18:05,496
Jaemin, what was your favorite school lunch menu?

532
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:07,760
- Vienna sausages. - Vienna sausages.

533
00:18:07,860 --> 00:18:09,806
- Those were good, right? - Wait, I want to know.

534
00:18:09,830 --> 00:18:11,406
What is usually served in Korean school lunches?

535
00:18:11,430 --> 00:18:13,830
- What is usually served? - Like, what food comes out often?

536
00:18:13,860 --> 00:18:16,499
Well, those drivers' diners you often visit?

537
00:18:16,500 --> 00:18:17,670
Things like that?

538
00:18:17,730 --> 00:18:19,470
Items from that menu are served often.

539
00:18:19,570 --> 00:18:21,239
Braised half-dried pollack pops up once in a while.

540
00:18:21,240 --> 00:18:23,369
(The explanation customized for BamBam is complete.)

541
00:18:23,370 --> 00:18:26,740
Honestly, I am really curious about BamBam and Carmen.

542
00:18:26,810 --> 00:18:28,550
- Did you get curry? - Like green curry?

543
00:18:28,610 --> 00:18:29,709
Green curry is served. Things like that.

544
00:18:29,710 --> 00:18:32,150
- I really love that. - Green curry, massaman...

545
00:18:32,220 --> 00:18:34,490
curry, and such. But...

546
00:18:34,690 --> 00:18:36,170
Do you know how spaghetti was served?

547
00:18:36,750 --> 00:18:40,260
Instead of beef tomato sauce, it was with krapow...

548
00:18:40,360 --> 00:18:41,666
- Krapow? - Do you know krapow moo saap?

549
00:18:41,690 --> 00:18:43,760
- What sauce is krapow moo saap? - What is that?

550
00:18:43,830 --> 00:18:47,060
Like basil, chili pepper, oyster sauce, and such.

551
00:18:47,160 --> 00:18:48,320
- Sounds good. - Sounds good.

552
00:18:48,370 --> 00:18:50,570
- It is stir-fried with that sauce. - Sounds good.

553
00:18:50,770 --> 00:18:52,270
People usually eat that with rice.

554
00:18:52,370 --> 00:18:54,740
Yes, so when I was a kid,

555
00:18:54,810 --> 00:18:57,470
I did not know spaghetti came with tomato sauce.

556
00:18:57,810 --> 00:18:59,679
That is fascinating.

557
00:18:59,680 --> 00:19:03,209
For me, I only had school lunches until kindergarten.

558
00:19:03,210 --> 00:19:04,680
Then what did you eat at lunchtime?

559
00:19:04,780 --> 00:19:07,356
- I just bought food at the canteen. - You bought food at the canteen?

560
00:19:07,380 --> 00:19:08,926
What they sell at the canteen must be obvious, though.

561
00:19:08,950 --> 00:19:11,020
There is a dish called nasi jinggo.

562
00:19:11,090 --> 00:19:12,320
- Nasi jinggo. - Right.

563
00:19:12,390 --> 00:19:14,750
It is a small lunch box packed with rice and side dishes...

564
00:19:14,860 --> 00:19:17,160
- wrapped in banana leaves. - That sounds delicious.

565
00:19:17,260 --> 00:19:18,860
Were there set menus like that too?

566
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:21,370
The set menu has Siomay and Batagor.

567
00:19:21,770 --> 00:19:25,840
- Siomay, siomay. - Siomay is kind of like dim sum.

568
00:19:25,900 --> 00:19:26,900
Like dumplings.

569
00:19:27,070 --> 00:19:28,146
- It differs from... - Is it different from...

570
00:19:28,170 --> 00:19:29,730
- siu mai from China? - It's different.

571
00:19:29,770 --> 00:19:32,540
The siu mai we know usually has pork and shrimp mixed together.

572
00:19:32,610 --> 00:19:35,010
But this is almost only fish,

573
00:19:35,080 --> 00:19:36,160
made entirely of fish meat.

574
00:19:36,580 --> 00:19:38,849
Then is it the same as shumai in Japan?

575
00:19:38,850 --> 00:19:39,979
- Right, like shumai. - Shumai.

576
00:19:39,980 --> 00:19:41,289
- It is similar. - So...

577
00:19:41,290 --> 00:19:43,450
shumai and siu mai spread over, and here,

578
00:19:43,850 --> 00:19:44,896
- it becomes siomay. - Siomay.

579
00:19:44,920 --> 00:19:45,966
- Here it is siomay. - Siomay.

580
00:19:45,990 --> 00:19:48,129
- Siomay, siu mai, and shumai. - The shapes are all similar,

581
00:19:48,130 --> 00:19:49,266
but there are slight differences in details.

582
00:19:49,290 --> 00:19:51,660
Here, it has no pork and only fish.

583
00:19:51,960 --> 00:19:54,570
- That is amazing. Fascinating. - Your knowledge is insane.

584
00:19:55,800 --> 00:19:57,546
I guess I'm the only one here who packed a lunch.

585
00:19:57,570 --> 00:19:59,516
- I brought a lunch too. - I also packed a lunch.

586
00:19:59,540 --> 00:20:01,076
- You brought one too? - I packed a lunch too.

587
00:20:01,100 --> 00:20:02,610
- Really? - My mom...

588
00:20:03,010 --> 00:20:04,910
That's a relief.

589
00:20:05,210 --> 00:20:07,186
Did you take packed lunches to school too, Minami?

590
00:20:07,210 --> 00:20:09,180
Yes, back in elementary school,

591
00:20:09,250 --> 00:20:12,980
- I only ate packed lunches. - Really?

592
00:20:13,080 --> 00:20:15,490
Then your parents packed that every single morning?

593
00:20:15,590 --> 00:20:17,419
- Yes, she woke up early. - Right.

594
00:20:17,420 --> 00:20:21,090
I have a younger sister, so she made one for my sister too.

595
00:20:21,160 --> 00:20:22,160
Wow.

596
00:20:22,690 --> 00:20:24,900
She made character bento boxes.

597
00:20:25,200 --> 00:20:29,070
- Cutting out the seaweed like that? - Yes. She cut out the seaweed.

598
00:20:29,130 --> 00:20:31,370
And using egg whites to make eyeballs...

599
00:20:31,440 --> 00:20:34,400
My mom used cheese for the whites of the eyes.

600
00:20:34,710 --> 00:20:38,280
- I bet everyone compared theirs. - We would see what others had...

601
00:20:38,340 --> 00:20:40,679
and ask our moms, "Can you make this for me too?"

602
00:20:40,680 --> 00:20:41,910
That kind of thing happened.

603
00:20:42,010 --> 00:20:44,349
To be a mom, you need an artistic sense.

604
00:20:44,350 --> 00:20:46,279
(Mom is the best!)

605
00:20:46,280 --> 00:20:48,390
Chenle, did you have a favorite lunch side dish?

606
00:20:48,690 --> 00:20:51,590
The most unique one was pigeon eggs.

607
00:20:51,890 --> 00:20:52,989
(What?)

608
00:20:52,990 --> 00:20:54,489
- Pigeon eggs? - What?

609
00:20:54,490 --> 00:20:55,759
- Nice. - I've never heard of that.

610
00:20:55,760 --> 00:20:58,800
They're really delicious. In China, they actually sell pigeon eggs.

611
00:20:59,100 --> 00:21:00,799
They're pretty common, like chicken eggs.

612
00:21:00,800 --> 00:21:02,900
Wait, do you boil them, eat them raw, or how?

613
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
You can boil them, or...

614
00:21:05,070 --> 00:21:06,576
- Crack them... - You can crack them open too.

615
00:21:06,600 --> 00:21:08,009
- Really? - Yes, try it.

616
00:21:08,010 --> 00:21:09,316
Do they taste similar to chicken eggs?

617
00:21:09,340 --> 00:21:10,369
They taste better than chicken eggs.

618
00:21:10,370 --> 00:21:11,816
- Really? - Way better than chicken eggs.

619
00:21:11,840 --> 00:21:14,610
When I was young, every single day, I ate two pigeon eggs.

620
00:21:14,810 --> 00:21:15,850
(Gosh)

621
00:21:15,950 --> 00:21:17,426
Having pigeon eggs as your favorite side dish...

622
00:21:17,450 --> 00:21:18,780
is so fascinating to me.

623
00:21:19,780 --> 00:21:22,090
(Chenle's love for pigeon eggs)

624
00:21:22,290 --> 00:21:25,990
Why don't we talk about the nostalgic snacks and foods...

625
00:21:26,090 --> 00:21:30,030
we used to eat often back in our school days?

626
00:21:30,090 --> 00:21:32,360
- Sounds good. - Back in my day,

627
00:21:32,430 --> 00:21:33,576
we called stationery stores "munbanggu."

628
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:34,700
Right, we had those.

629
00:21:35,200 --> 00:21:36,800
(The hot spot in front of school)

630
00:21:37,200 --> 00:21:38,560
(The go-to hangout spot back then)

631
00:21:38,800 --> 00:21:42,270
They were stationery stores, but they also sold food and drinks.

632
00:21:42,340 --> 00:21:45,180
- Slushies. - They sold slushies, and...

633
00:21:45,240 --> 00:21:46,840
the owner would suddenly make dalgona...

634
00:21:46,910 --> 00:21:48,249
Make dalgona, grill dried fish fillets...

635
00:21:48,250 --> 00:21:50,909
Grilling dried fish fillets. They just did everything.

636
00:21:50,910 --> 00:21:52,156
(Shop owners who were all-rounders)

637
00:21:52,180 --> 00:21:53,950
In Bali, did you have street food...

638
00:21:54,020 --> 00:21:56,190
- or snacks like that? - So many.

639
00:21:56,290 --> 00:21:57,949
- There's a food called Cilok. - Cilok?

640
00:21:57,950 --> 00:21:59,990
It's similar to Bakso.

641
00:22:00,290 --> 00:22:03,090
- It's made from tapioca starch. - Kind of like tteokbokki?

642
00:22:03,490 --> 00:22:07,500
Yes, chewy like tteokbokki. They put cilok in a plastic bag,

643
00:22:07,900 --> 00:22:10,100
- tie up the top. - Tie up?

644
00:22:10,500 --> 00:22:13,340
- And then at the bottom here, - At the bottom?

645
00:22:13,400 --> 00:22:18,910
- you bite it to make a hole, - Bite the plastic to make a hole?

646
00:22:19,010 --> 00:22:22,080
And eat it through there. Seriously. It is so good.

647
00:22:22,150 --> 00:22:23,150
That is how you eat it.

648
00:22:23,151 --> 00:22:24,919
(Munching)

649
00:22:24,920 --> 00:22:25,920
Right.

650
00:22:25,980 --> 00:22:28,790
For sweets, there are Dadar Gulung and Onde-onde,

651
00:22:28,990 --> 00:22:30,790
which are kind of like sesame balls.

652
00:22:30,890 --> 00:22:32,130
They are like sweet rice balls,

653
00:22:32,290 --> 00:22:35,090
- covered in sesame seeds, you know? - Right, covered in sesame seeds.

654
00:22:35,790 --> 00:22:38,460
Dadar Gulung has a thin batter,

655
00:22:38,530 --> 00:22:40,006
and is filled with sugar and coconut.

656
00:22:40,030 --> 00:22:41,129
- With pandan leaves, - Right.

657
00:22:41,130 --> 00:22:42,276
- so it is kind of green. - With pandan leaves.

658
00:22:42,300 --> 00:22:44,970
- Exactly. - Then in Thailand,

659
00:22:45,040 --> 00:22:46,316
what kind of snacks did you eat at school?

660
00:22:46,340 --> 00:22:48,710
- You definitely have to buy Coke. - Coke?

661
00:22:48,770 --> 00:22:52,339
Put ice in a plastic bag, drink it with a straw.

662
00:22:52,340 --> 00:22:57,779
And corn in a cup, mixed with butter and sugar.

663
00:22:57,780 --> 00:22:59,656
- You know that, right? - Condensed milk. Condensed milk.

664
00:22:59,680 --> 00:23:00,840
Yes, condensed milk. Exactly.

665
00:23:00,880 --> 00:23:03,090
- They sell that. - It is so delicious.

666
00:23:03,790 --> 00:23:06,160
They served a lot in cups. We also have cup tteokbokki.

667
00:23:06,220 --> 00:23:07,990
- Cupbokki. - Cupbokki.

668
00:23:08,090 --> 00:23:09,260
Because of cupbokki,

669
00:23:09,330 --> 00:23:11,830
- I got so good at riding a bike. - Why? How?

670
00:23:11,900 --> 00:23:14,000
Because I had to eat cupbokki on the go,

671
00:23:14,100 --> 00:23:15,400
I rode hands-free...

672
00:23:16,200 --> 00:23:17,776
- Holding and eating while riding? - I ate while riding.

673
00:23:17,800 --> 00:23:20,240
So I steered the bike just by turning my body.

674
00:23:20,400 --> 00:23:22,970
- Like a unicycle, right? - You must be good at it.

675
00:23:23,040 --> 00:23:24,910
So I always rode my bike hands-free.

676
00:23:25,010 --> 00:23:26,730
- No way. - I bet Minami could do that too.

677
00:23:26,910 --> 00:23:28,610
Then I could hold cupbokki...

678
00:23:29,250 --> 00:23:31,850
- Gosh. - In Japan, are there...

679
00:23:31,920 --> 00:23:32,920
school snacks like that?

680
00:23:32,980 --> 00:23:35,650
Not really at school, but...

681
00:23:35,720 --> 00:23:38,920
- when going out, there is jubako... - Jubako?

682
00:23:38,990 --> 00:23:41,629
There are lunch boxes about this big,

683
00:23:41,630 --> 00:23:44,060
stacked 2 or 3 layers high.

684
00:23:44,130 --> 00:23:47,700
Things like katsu sando were packed a lot.

685
00:23:48,300 --> 00:23:51,099
Because "katsu" in Japanese means "to win."

686
00:23:51,100 --> 00:23:53,800
I see, they are homophones.

687
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,070
Like, eat this and win, that kind of thing.

688
00:23:56,140 --> 00:23:57,780
- Win at sports day. - Win at sports day.

689
00:23:58,380 --> 00:24:00,220
(BamBam is serious, thinking about something.)

690
00:24:01,010 --> 00:24:02,310
Then what is tonkatsu?

691
00:24:02,610 --> 00:24:05,280
Well, "ton" means pig or pork...

692
00:24:05,780 --> 00:24:06,980
You beat the pig?

693
00:24:07,420 --> 00:24:08,420
(What do you mean?)

694
00:24:08,890 --> 00:24:10,649
- It is just the same word. - The same word?

695
00:24:10,650 --> 00:24:12,690
It sounds the same.

696
00:24:13,560 --> 00:24:14,560
Defeat the pig.

697
00:24:15,190 --> 00:24:16,530
(Laughing)

698
00:24:16,590 --> 00:24:20,160
Talking about school food could really go on forever,

699
00:24:20,230 --> 00:24:22,200
but now, with delicious food,

700
00:24:22,270 --> 00:24:23,790
- Finally. - we can make new memories.

701
00:24:24,130 --> 00:24:25,140
Finally.

702
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:26,639
- I am so hungry. - So hungry.

703
00:24:26,640 --> 00:24:29,839
It is time for our Special Recipe.

704
00:24:29,840 --> 00:24:31,210
- Hooray! - Finally.

705
00:24:31,840 --> 00:24:33,839
- Who is cooking today? - Sung Woon prepared something.

706
00:24:33,840 --> 00:24:36,110
Yes, I brought a recipe.

707
00:24:36,210 --> 00:24:37,610
(Chef Cloud's dish is coming soon.)

708
00:24:37,710 --> 00:24:39,750
It is super simple. It takes just ten minutes...

709
00:24:39,820 --> 00:24:41,650
- to whip up. - Nice.

710
00:24:41,720 --> 00:24:43,450
It is still great to make and eat.

711
00:24:43,520 --> 00:24:45,290
A real comfort food from your school days.

712
00:24:45,490 --> 00:24:47,290
It's recreated in the 2026 version.

713
00:24:49,390 --> 00:24:51,659
I have the perfect delicious dish. Something I love to eat.

714
00:24:51,660 --> 00:24:52,699
How long does it take?

715
00:24:52,700 --> 00:24:53,829
- Ten minutes. - It only takes ten minutes?

716
00:24:53,830 --> 00:24:54,900
Is it possible?

717
00:24:55,100 --> 00:24:57,400
It takes less than ten minutes. Who would believe...

718
00:24:57,470 --> 00:24:58,676
- It looks great. - this took ten minutes?

719
00:24:58,700 --> 00:24:59,899
Sung Woon, come on in.

720
00:24:59,900 --> 00:25:03,370
(An explosive reaction as soon as it opens)

721
00:25:03,510 --> 00:25:04,510
(Delicious!)

722
00:25:04,511 --> 00:25:05,591
I think it is so delicious.

723
00:25:05,910 --> 00:25:07,010
I would make this at home.

724
00:25:07,310 --> 00:25:09,010
What if this was served for school lunch?

725
00:25:09,110 --> 00:25:10,810
No student would hate this menu.

726
00:25:10,880 --> 00:25:13,200
(Today's dish which captured the students is coming soon.)

727
00:25:13,350 --> 00:25:14,350
In 2026.

728
00:25:16,090 --> 00:25:17,190
(Shining brighter on stage)

729
00:25:17,550 --> 00:25:20,590
(Wanna One's Ha Sung Woon)

730
00:25:22,360 --> 00:25:23,360
Going back in time.

731
00:25:23,890 --> 00:25:24,900
Going back.

732
00:25:25,800 --> 00:25:26,929
- In 2010? - In 2010?

733
00:25:26,930 --> 00:25:28,729
It was when I first came to Korea.

734
00:25:28,730 --> 00:25:30,630
(My school days in 2010)

735
00:25:32,600 --> 00:25:36,170
In 2010, 17-year-old Sung Woon is still running...

736
00:25:36,240 --> 00:25:38,710
toward his dream of becoming an idol.

737
00:25:39,510 --> 00:25:40,610
He was 17.

738
00:25:40,710 --> 00:25:42,410
(Feeling shy for no reason)

739
00:25:43,710 --> 00:25:46,180
(01. A day in the life of trainee Sung Woon)

740
00:25:47,350 --> 00:25:48,350
Hello.

741
00:25:48,490 --> 00:25:50,590
I am a 17-year-old trainee, Ha Sung Woon.

742
00:25:50,690 --> 00:25:53,419
I am going to show you my daily routine today.

743
00:25:53,420 --> 00:25:54,760
Aren't you curious?

744
00:25:55,090 --> 00:25:56,290
Starting with the basics.

745
00:25:56,590 --> 00:25:57,889
(Dancing)

746
00:25:57,890 --> 00:25:59,400
- Oh, yes. - Up and down.

747
00:26:01,100 --> 00:26:02,676
- Get into position. - You've got a text.

748
00:26:02,700 --> 00:26:03,700
You've got a text.

749
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:06,176
(Yoon Ji Sung: I'm coming over. I'm hungry.)

750
00:26:06,200 --> 00:26:09,540
Why is Yoon Ji Sung coming again? He's hungry?

751
00:26:09,710 --> 00:26:11,186
Wait, he said he was out of texting credits...

752
00:26:11,210 --> 00:26:12,290
Did he borrow some credits?

753
00:26:12,710 --> 00:26:15,309
I remember we were using smartphones ten years ago.

754
00:26:15,310 --> 00:26:16,610
(You're right.)

755
00:26:16,910 --> 00:26:18,480
This feels almost like the 1990s.

756
00:26:19,280 --> 00:26:21,380
Two hours later.

757
00:26:21,990 --> 00:26:23,990
Sung Woon, let's hang out.

758
00:26:24,790 --> 00:26:25,790
Ji Sung.

759
00:26:26,120 --> 00:26:27,120
Sung Woon.

760
00:26:27,190 --> 00:26:28,606
What are you doing here at this hour?

761
00:26:28,630 --> 00:26:31,900
- You told me to come. - You said you were coming.

762
00:26:32,460 --> 00:26:34,829
- Aren't we friends? Oh, right. - Right.

763
00:26:34,830 --> 00:26:37,000
- Hey, get a grip. - Yes, I should get a grip.

764
00:26:37,500 --> 00:26:39,000
Hey, but what about food?

765
00:26:39,600 --> 00:26:40,720
Did you come without eating?

766
00:26:40,900 --> 00:26:42,546
I usually don't eat before going to someone's house.

767
00:26:42,570 --> 00:26:44,640
- Why? - I'm just kind of rude like that.

768
00:26:45,410 --> 00:26:46,809
(Who do you think you are?)

769
00:26:46,810 --> 00:26:49,880
My style is to come a bit starved. That way, I can kind of judge...

770
00:26:49,950 --> 00:26:52,079
their cooking skills or hospitality.

771
00:26:52,080 --> 00:26:53,596
I tend to check out that kind of stuff.

772
00:26:53,620 --> 00:26:56,950
Then for today, I have the perfect delicious dish.

773
00:26:57,020 --> 00:26:58,020
Something I love to eat.

774
00:26:58,420 --> 00:26:59,520
Isn't it omakase today?

775
00:26:59,620 --> 00:27:02,890
- I came expecting omakase. - A kid like you, wanting omakase?

776
00:27:03,090 --> 00:27:04,490
How do you even know about omakase?

777
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:06,860
- How old are we right now? - We're 17.

778
00:27:06,930 --> 00:27:08,446
Right, that's true. You're 17 right now...

779
00:27:08,470 --> 00:27:10,700
- We're 17 years old? - A 17-year-old having omakase?

780
00:27:10,830 --> 00:27:11,876
Hey, let's just keep it simple.

781
00:27:11,900 --> 00:27:13,076
- Yes, hey. - Let's keep it simple.

782
00:27:13,100 --> 00:27:15,710
Hold on a second. It should be in the fridge.

783
00:27:16,270 --> 00:27:18,010
- Don't be shocked. - All right.

784
00:27:18,070 --> 00:27:19,910
There's something amazing in here.

785
00:27:20,440 --> 00:27:24,250
Are we playing hide-and-seek? So shiny.

786
00:27:24,310 --> 00:27:26,780
(So shiny)

787
00:27:27,380 --> 00:27:28,390
What's this?

788
00:27:28,450 --> 00:27:31,420
Look. Look at this. See what's inside.

789
00:27:32,060 --> 00:27:34,190
- Why? - There's nothing here.

790
00:27:34,260 --> 00:27:36,730
Kimchi? There's only kimchi.

791
00:27:36,790 --> 00:27:37,790
Man, I'm going home.

792
00:27:38,330 --> 00:27:40,576
How can there be only kimchi after inviting someone over? Right?

793
00:27:40,600 --> 00:27:43,600
- Kimchi and rice are all you need. - Right.

794
00:27:43,700 --> 00:27:45,770
- That's the tastiest. - That's true.

795
00:27:46,140 --> 00:27:48,510
What's this? Why is the rice so tiny?

796
00:27:49,410 --> 00:27:50,640
Just because you're small,

797
00:27:50,710 --> 00:27:52,386
you're telling me to eat tiny portions of rice?

798
00:27:52,410 --> 00:27:53,640
How can we eat like this?

799
00:27:53,710 --> 00:27:55,086
When will we grow up and become idols?

800
00:27:55,110 --> 00:27:57,110
- This is plenty big. - Really?

801
00:27:57,180 --> 00:27:59,596
- This rice is way too cute, though. - Hey, this isn't all we have.

802
00:27:59,620 --> 00:28:01,896
- Then what else is there? - There's something in the freezer.

803
00:28:01,920 --> 00:28:05,060
- Guess what? Steamed dumplings. - Steamed dumplings?

804
00:28:05,320 --> 00:28:07,096
- My nickname is Dumpling Monster. - Dumplings.

805
00:28:07,120 --> 00:28:08,196
I'm totally a dumpling freak.

806
00:28:08,220 --> 00:28:10,890
Hey, have you ever seen this before? This mini-sized rice.

807
00:28:11,160 --> 00:28:12,160
I've never seen it.

808
00:28:12,300 --> 00:28:14,100
It's the perfect light portion to eat.

809
00:28:14,300 --> 00:28:15,406
So honestly, when you're short on time,

810
00:28:15,430 --> 00:28:16,506
with this kimchi, dumplings,

811
00:28:16,530 --> 00:28:18,770
and this mini rice, these three things are all you need.

812
00:28:18,870 --> 00:28:19,870
Okay, got it.

813
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:23,610
Wait, aren't the ingredients a bit too simple?

814
00:28:23,810 --> 00:28:25,610
Dumplings, kimchi, and instant rice.

815
00:28:25,810 --> 00:28:28,809
- You stuff the rice in dumplings. - No, no.

816
00:28:28,810 --> 00:28:31,580
Then putting dumplings in kimchi soup with rice?

817
00:28:31,650 --> 00:28:34,320
- Nope. - Rice, dumplings, and kimchi on it?

818
00:28:34,780 --> 00:28:37,050
Wait, that's pretty close.

819
00:28:37,920 --> 00:28:40,220
- Or... - The combination is actually good.

820
00:28:40,290 --> 00:28:41,860
It has to be delicious already.

821
00:28:41,930 --> 00:28:43,690
- So what are you making me? - This is...

822
00:28:43,890 --> 00:28:46,000
Dumpling, kimchi, rice.

823
00:28:46,500 --> 00:28:48,700
- Dumpling, kimchi, rice. - Dumpling, kimchi, rice.

824
00:28:49,100 --> 00:28:50,776
Don't separate them, it's one. Dumpling Kimchi Rice.

825
00:28:50,800 --> 00:28:52,239
- So basically, they're a group. - Yes, exactly.

826
00:28:52,240 --> 00:28:53,839
- Like us in ten years? - Yes.

827
00:28:53,840 --> 00:28:55,440
Alright, fine. Just feed me.

828
00:28:55,510 --> 00:28:56,870
- Okay. - Give me some food.

829
00:28:57,010 --> 00:28:58,686
Wait, come to think of it, you don't even have a stove.

830
00:28:58,710 --> 00:29:01,810
I may not be good at cooking, but with just a microwave,

831
00:29:01,910 --> 00:29:03,510
I can become a chef.

832
00:29:03,610 --> 00:29:07,219
(A microwave?)

833
00:29:07,220 --> 00:29:10,619
(Come to think of it, there's only microwave.)

834
00:29:10,620 --> 00:29:11,620
In a microwave?

835
00:29:12,020 --> 00:29:13,896
Cooking without heat? I'm not eating. So annoying.

836
00:29:13,920 --> 00:29:15,090
Come on, in the microwave,

837
00:29:15,160 --> 00:29:16,266
you can totally heat this up and eat it.

838
00:29:16,290 --> 00:29:19,200
- I want something cooked over fire. - Just trust me on this.

839
00:29:19,760 --> 00:29:21,900
- How long will it take? - Ten minutes.

840
00:29:22,700 --> 00:29:23,976
- I can cook it in ten minutes, - Is it possible?

841
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:26,440
- How can you cook in ten minutes? - I'll show you.

842
00:29:26,500 --> 00:29:27,500
Seriously, trust me.

843
00:29:28,200 --> 00:29:30,110
You came to my place, so just trust me.

844
00:29:31,910 --> 00:29:33,110
Alright, let's get started.

845
00:29:33,210 --> 00:29:36,250
As you can see, the ingredients are already prepared.

846
00:29:36,310 --> 00:29:37,756
You prepped everything just to show off, right?

847
00:29:37,780 --> 00:29:39,719
- Yes, I started prepping yesterday. - Did you?

848
00:29:39,720 --> 00:29:41,579
- I prepared them thoroughly. - This is the omakase.

849
00:29:41,580 --> 00:29:43,149
(The ingredients of Dumpling Kimchi Rice)

850
00:29:43,150 --> 00:29:45,720
It's really simple. Not complicated at all.

851
00:29:46,520 --> 00:29:47,836
- Truly ten minutes without fire? - Yes.

852
00:29:47,860 --> 00:29:50,190
- Time it. - I'm setting a timer right now.

853
00:29:50,260 --> 00:29:52,400
- Go ahead, time it. - Okay. Ten minutes, right?

854
00:29:52,560 --> 00:29:53,606
- Alright. - Ten minutes starts now.

855
00:29:53,630 --> 00:29:55,130
(The 10-minute timer starts now.)

856
00:29:55,200 --> 00:29:56,300
Ten minutes starts now.

857
00:29:56,700 --> 00:29:57,799
Beat the egg first?

858
00:29:57,800 --> 00:30:00,739
(Put 1 egg into the bowl.)

859
00:30:00,740 --> 00:30:02,820
- Should I stir it? Add the rice? - Put the rice in.

860
00:30:04,510 --> 00:30:07,480
- Put the rice in. - Egg goes in, then the rice.

861
00:30:07,540 --> 00:30:08,550
Add the rice.

862
00:30:08,850 --> 00:30:12,120
Next, black pepper. We'll add pepper and oyster sauce.

863
00:30:12,520 --> 00:30:13,949
Anything with oyster sauce tastes good.

864
00:30:13,950 --> 00:30:14,979
You're right.

865
00:30:14,980 --> 00:30:16,496
- And then... - Should I break up all the rice?

866
00:30:16,520 --> 00:30:17,520
Yes.

867
00:30:19,120 --> 00:30:20,836
Are you sure this will be done in ten minutes?

868
00:30:20,860 --> 00:30:22,430
- It will. - Is this all I have to do?

869
00:30:22,490 --> 00:30:24,760
- Yes, mash it more. - That way,

870
00:30:24,830 --> 00:30:26,930
the grains will soak up the beaten egg.

871
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:28,000
That's right, exactly.

872
00:30:29,100 --> 00:30:30,200
I'll pour some perilla oil.

873
00:30:31,100 --> 00:30:33,100
- I can't live without perilla oil. - Me neither.

874
00:30:33,170 --> 00:30:34,610
You know how delicious it is, right?

875
00:30:35,410 --> 00:30:37,469
- It is so good. - It must smell amazing.

876
00:30:37,470 --> 00:30:38,939
Yes.

877
00:30:38,940 --> 00:30:42,110
So the perilla oil coats it evenly...

878
00:30:42,210 --> 00:30:43,310
To get it coated evenly.

879
00:30:44,510 --> 00:30:45,520
You are so smart.

880
00:30:46,220 --> 00:30:47,700
Do not go around sharing this recipe.

881
00:30:47,750 --> 00:30:48,750
Okay.

882
00:30:49,020 --> 00:30:50,940
Wait, Sung Woon, you said you would cook for me,

883
00:30:51,450 --> 00:30:52,819
(Feeling guilty)

884
00:30:52,820 --> 00:30:55,020
- but I am doing all the cooking. - No, you are not.

885
00:30:55,090 --> 00:30:57,190
Well, it tastes better when we make it together.

886
00:30:57,660 --> 00:30:59,760
- Is it mashed up enough? - Yes. That is good.

887
00:31:00,900 --> 00:31:02,469
But almost six minutes have passed already,

888
00:31:02,470 --> 00:31:03,500
so I am slowly...

889
00:31:04,100 --> 00:31:05,100
reaching my limit.

890
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:08,770
I get hangry, so in ten minutes... You better watch out.

891
00:31:08,840 --> 00:31:11,170
- Hey, is this all you do? - Just like that.

892
00:31:11,510 --> 00:31:13,910
Are they making kimchi risotto with dumplings?

893
00:31:15,210 --> 00:31:17,610
Now microwave it with these dumplings.

894
00:31:17,810 --> 00:31:19,596
Tear the corner slightly. You know the drill,

895
00:31:19,620 --> 00:31:20,620
from the military.

896
00:31:21,020 --> 00:31:23,490
Totally. In the military, when you want them juicy,

897
00:31:23,550 --> 00:31:25,496
- you rip open this corner, right? - Just a little, just a little.

898
00:31:25,520 --> 00:31:27,036
Just a little. Hey, I already opened it.

899
00:31:27,060 --> 00:31:28,060
This is fine.

900
00:31:28,120 --> 00:31:29,129
Just tear it slightly.

901
00:31:29,130 --> 00:31:31,059
Wait, did you serve in the military? At 17?

902
00:31:31,060 --> 00:31:33,599
(Their world is about to collapse.)

903
00:31:33,600 --> 00:31:34,900
I actually lied about my age.

904
00:31:36,400 --> 00:31:37,446
Wait, do we just microwave it now?

905
00:31:37,470 --> 00:31:39,470
- Just like this. - All right.

906
00:31:39,870 --> 00:31:41,540
Turn out delicious.

907
00:31:42,140 --> 00:31:44,210
(Ji Sung's camera)

908
00:31:44,510 --> 00:31:46,609
You microwave them together? I see.

909
00:31:46,610 --> 00:31:48,626
- Two minutes and forty seconds. - Two minutes and forty seconds?

910
00:31:48,650 --> 00:31:50,370
- So detailed. - Details, details, details.

911
00:31:50,410 --> 00:31:52,020
(While the microwave runs...)

912
00:31:52,220 --> 00:31:54,720
- Basic moves, basic moves. - Isolations.

913
00:31:55,920 --> 00:31:57,420
(Dancing machine)

914
00:31:57,750 --> 00:32:00,119
Look at you. Fancy moves.

915
00:32:00,120 --> 00:32:01,519
(Fancy moves)

916
00:32:01,520 --> 00:32:04,090
Keep that up and you might debut as an idol.

917
00:32:06,430 --> 00:32:07,499
Is it done?

918
00:32:07,500 --> 00:32:08,536
Two minutes and forty seconds are up.

919
00:32:08,560 --> 00:32:10,570
- Yes. Is it ready? - Gosh.

920
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:12,300
Nice.

921
00:32:13,700 --> 00:32:15,840
- What is this? - The rice turned golden brown.

922
00:32:15,910 --> 00:32:17,970
- It became rice with egg, right? - Yes.

923
00:32:18,470 --> 00:32:19,480
The smell is insane.

924
00:32:20,710 --> 00:32:23,910
- It really steamed perfectly. - Right in here.

925
00:32:24,410 --> 00:32:25,420
Tuck it in.

926
00:32:25,720 --> 00:32:26,720
Hey.

927
00:32:26,721 --> 00:32:28,519
(Gosh, it's so hot!)

928
00:32:28,520 --> 00:32:29,896
(His hands were faster than the heat.)

929
00:32:29,920 --> 00:32:30,920
Hold on.

930
00:32:31,650 --> 00:32:32,850
Let me let it cool down a bit.

931
00:32:34,660 --> 00:32:35,660
Hold on.

932
00:32:36,390 --> 00:32:37,390
I'll try it again.

933
00:32:37,790 --> 00:32:39,400
- This looks good. - Looks delicious.

934
00:32:39,460 --> 00:32:42,000
- How is it? How is it? - It is so juicy.

935
00:32:42,100 --> 00:32:44,529
Like freshly steamed dumplings,

936
00:32:44,530 --> 00:32:46,069
- they feel really juicy, right? - Totally.

937
00:32:46,070 --> 00:32:48,500
It is great for a light bite when you are hungry.

938
00:32:49,610 --> 00:32:51,246
Dude, you really do not have much time left. Seriously.

939
00:32:51,270 --> 00:32:52,280
How much...

940
00:32:52,340 --> 00:32:54,440
Once ten minutes are up, the phone will blow up.

941
00:32:54,510 --> 00:32:56,756
- All right, I will be quick. - You made such a big deal about it.

942
00:32:56,780 --> 00:33:00,149
Okay, almost done. Now in here, I'll add...

943
00:33:00,150 --> 00:33:01,379
two-thirds of fried kimchi.

944
00:33:01,380 --> 00:33:02,719
- Two-thirds? - Yes.

945
00:33:02,720 --> 00:33:04,650
This is stir-fried kimchi, right?

946
00:33:04,720 --> 00:33:05,759
- Yes, stir-fried. - Add the stir-fried kimchi.

947
00:33:05,760 --> 00:33:07,059
- That's stir-fried kimchi. - Looks so good.

948
00:33:07,060 --> 00:33:08,119
He is adding stir-fried kimchi.

949
00:33:08,120 --> 00:33:10,136
- That is going to be tasty, though. - This has got to be good.

950
00:33:10,160 --> 00:33:11,366
This is the real deal, honestly.

951
00:33:11,390 --> 00:33:13,310
- Hey, can I try a piece of kimchi? - Want some?

952
00:33:13,360 --> 00:33:14,700
Yes, I am so hungry.

953
00:33:14,800 --> 00:33:16,400
- I need to whet my appetite. - Grab it.

954
00:33:16,800 --> 00:33:17,870
(Yum)

955
00:33:19,240 --> 00:33:21,970
- It is really crunchy. - Yes, but this kimchi...

956
00:33:22,040 --> 00:33:23,920
was stir-fried in a cauldron over an open fire.

957
00:33:25,410 --> 00:33:26,486
You taste that smoky flavor, right?

958
00:33:26,510 --> 00:33:28,086
It feels like it's stir-frying in my mouth.

959
00:33:28,110 --> 00:33:29,209
Wait a minute.

960
00:33:29,210 --> 00:33:31,050
(He's startled.)

961
00:33:33,420 --> 00:33:35,196
It is so hot. The fire flavor burned my tongue.

962
00:33:35,220 --> 00:33:37,096
- Seriously? - The fire flavor burned my tongue.

963
00:33:37,120 --> 00:33:38,490
- Gosh. - Should I ease up?

964
00:33:38,550 --> 00:33:40,136
- You are good at acting. - Should I ease up?

965
00:33:40,160 --> 00:33:41,160
Nice job.

966
00:33:41,161 --> 00:33:43,390
(What kind of dish will he make?)

967
00:33:43,790 --> 00:33:45,660
- So with the dumplings... - Yes.

968
00:33:45,730 --> 00:33:47,170
I am going to put about five on top.

969
00:33:48,000 --> 00:33:50,169
(Dumpling Kimchi Rice Step 7: Place dumplings and press gently.)

970
00:33:50,170 --> 00:33:52,269
(Plump)

971
00:33:52,270 --> 00:33:53,270
He placed them nicely.

972
00:33:54,500 --> 00:33:56,260
Next up is this. You know what it is, right?

973
00:33:56,910 --> 00:33:58,880
- Mozzarella cheese. - Yes.

974
00:33:58,980 --> 00:34:00,356
- Adding mozzarella cheese? - Yes, you have to.

975
00:34:00,380 --> 00:34:01,610
Okay. Sprinkle it.

976
00:34:02,850 --> 00:34:04,580
- Cheese. - Mozzarella cheese.

977
00:34:04,710 --> 00:34:05,719
He used a cheat code.

978
00:34:05,720 --> 00:34:07,320
(Sprinkling cheese)

979
00:34:07,920 --> 00:34:10,220
- Pizza? - It looks like pizza.

980
00:34:10,620 --> 00:34:12,590
After this, we cover it with plastic wrap.

981
00:34:12,660 --> 00:34:13,660
Wrap...

982
00:34:13,661 --> 00:34:15,259
- Wrap! - Wrap!

983
00:34:15,260 --> 00:34:16,730
(Their energy explodes.)

984
00:34:17,030 --> 00:34:18,059
Wrap, pizza, cheese

985
00:34:18,060 --> 00:34:19,360
Sprinkle over kimchi

986
00:34:19,560 --> 00:34:23,369
- Yo! - Cover with wrap! Poke, poke, poke!

987
00:34:23,370 --> 00:34:24,599
(Feeling proud)

988
00:34:24,600 --> 00:34:25,999
Am I getting paid for this appearance?

989
00:34:26,000 --> 00:34:28,810
- They'd pay me with dumplings. - Really?

990
00:34:29,410 --> 00:34:30,640
Now, should I make holes?

991
00:34:30,710 --> 00:34:31,710
Right.

992
00:34:31,810 --> 00:34:34,409
Poke, poke, poke, poke, poke, poke, poke, poke, poke, poke.

993
00:34:34,410 --> 00:34:35,580
Hey.

994
00:34:36,010 --> 00:34:37,356
Ji Sung, you almost stabbed the dumpling.

995
00:34:37,380 --> 00:34:38,950
- Really? - Yes.

996
00:34:39,020 --> 00:34:40,196
It needs to be heated in the microwave again.

997
00:34:40,220 --> 00:34:41,356
The cheese will be completely melted.

998
00:34:41,380 --> 00:34:42,780
- And then, three minutes? - Right.

999
00:34:43,190 --> 00:34:45,560
(He sets a 3-minute timer.)

1000
00:34:45,860 --> 00:34:46,959
(It's time to brag their beauty while the microwave is working.)

1001
00:34:46,960 --> 00:34:47,960
What do you think?

1002
00:34:48,020 --> 00:34:49,420
This brings back so much nostalgia.

1003
00:34:49,930 --> 00:34:51,006
You know, we are in the old days.

1004
00:34:51,030 --> 00:34:53,230
By older, what do you mean? Late Joseon era?

1005
00:34:54,100 --> 00:34:55,676
Are we talking the Korean War era or what?

1006
00:34:55,700 --> 00:34:56,700
About my age...

1007
00:34:56,800 --> 00:34:58,006
It turns out I'm a bit sensitive about my age.

1008
00:34:58,030 --> 00:34:59,470
It's coming out really well, though.

1009
00:35:02,410 --> 00:35:04,010
- Finally. - Is it done?

1010
00:35:04,510 --> 00:35:07,010
It's just about to be finished.

1011
00:35:07,480 --> 00:35:10,749
(Full of melted cheese)

1012
00:35:10,750 --> 00:35:12,920
- That looks so good. - It really looks delicious.

1013
00:35:13,020 --> 00:35:15,720
Doesn't it look amazing? Wait, we haven't done this part yet.

1014
